text:
describe the big facts behind the questions of macroeconomics, using real data for many countries;
explain the structure of an economic model: parameters and exogenous variables go in, and endogenous variables come out;
solve the supply-and-demand model of the labor market and run experiments with it;
tell the long run (economic growth) from the short run (fluctuations around potential output);
load data tables with pandas, check them, and draw interactive line charts with Plotly;
write short Python functions and turn them into interactive figures with sliders.

explain why production = expenditure = income, and split GDP with the national income identity \(Y = C + I + G + NX\);
describe how the composition of U.S. GDP and labor’s share of income have changed since 1929;
separate nominal from real GDP with Laspeyres, Paasche and chain-weighted indexes, and measure inflation with the GDP deflator;
compare GDP across countries with exchange rates and purchasing power parity;
read CSV files with pandas, compute new columns, work with dates, and draw area and line charts with Plotly Express.

describe how recent sustained growth is, and how unevenly it spread across countries;
use the constant growth rule \(y_t = y_0(1+\bar{g})^t\) and the Rule of 70 for doubling times;
read a ratio scale, on which constant growth is a straight line;
compute an average annual growth rate between any two years;
apply the rules for growth rates of products, ratios and powers;
reshape, filter and summarize data tables with pandas, and draw line charts, scatter plots and step charts with Plotly Express.

write down the Cobb-Douglas production function and explain constant returns to scale and diminishing marginal products;
solve the production model: find the equilibrium wage, rental price of capital and output, and show that labor gets two-thirds of output and capital one-third;
compare the model’s predictions with data on 111 countries, a method called development accounting;
measure total factor productivity (TFP) as the part of income the model cannot explain with capital;
write Python functions with several arguments, combine Plotly Express charts with model curves, and select, filter and summarize rows of a large data table with pandas.

draw the Solow diagram and explain why an economy moves toward a steady state;
compute the steady-state levels of capital and output per person from the model’s parameters;
run experiments: a higher investment rate, a higher depreciation rate, faster population growth, or an earthquake that destroys capital;
use the principle of transition dynamics to read cross-country data on growth and investment;
write short Python functions, draw interactive figures with Plotly, and work with data tables in pandas.
